---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const zrows = [
  ["South Africa", "616", "1:87,951", "10,814"],
  ["Australia", "16", "1:1,687,231", "81,699"],
  ["United Arab Emirates", "6", "1:1,527,046", "51,993"],
  ["United States", "3", "1:120,819,644", "1,331,040"],
  ["Botswana", "1", "1:2,186,929", "30,250"],
  ["Ireland", "1", "1:4,708,939", "29,543"],
  ["England", "1", "1:55,718,059", "489,080"],
  ["Saudi Arabia", "1", "1:30,855,817", "63,028"],
  ["Turkey", "1", "1:77,821,422", "191,047"],
];
const srows = [
  ["South Africa", "52,009", "1:1,042", "—"],
  ["Namibia", "1,020", "—", "—"],
  ["England", "175", "—", "—"],
  ["Saudi Arabia", "159", "—", "—"],
  ["Australia", "99", "—", "—"],
  ["United States", "77", "—", "—"],
];
---
<Base title="The name"
      eyebrow="646 in the world, against 53,764"
      dek="Booyzen is not a variant spelling that drifted. It is one family's deliberate break from a mass surname — and the distribution figures, gathered a century later and entirely independently of the family, say exactly that.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The claim being tested</h2>
  <blockquote>
    "There were some Booysens who were joiners, fighting on the side of the British, so our great
    grandfather <strong>changed the spelling to Booyzen</strong> so as not to be associated with them."
    <span class="cap" style="display:block;margin-top:8px">— family testimony recorded on the MyHeritage
    tree from Tersia Booyzen. <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The full account &rarr;</a></span>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> That a change happened is on the record twice over. MyHeritage
    carries <strong>"Former name: Booysen"</strong> as a formal Name Change fact on
    <strong>Willem Hermanus "William Henry" Booyzen (1851–1905)</strong> — and, far better, both spellings
    appear <strong>on a single sheet of paper in September 1905</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:22px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-clerk-booysen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1905 death form, clerk's hand, reading Willem Hermanus Booysen with an s." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The registering clerk names the dead man:
        <strong>Willem Hermanus Boo<em>y</em>sen</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-signature-booyzen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1905 death form, informant signature reading J B Booyzen with a clear z." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Four lines below, his son signs:
        <strong>J. B. Boo<em>y</em><em>z</em>en</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The informant is <strong>John Barry Booyzen</strong>, the eldest son, twenty-one, recorded as
    <em>present at the death</em>. His father is buried under the old spelling; he signs the new one.
    <a href={u("/documents/")}>The full document &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip lore">Family lore</span> The <em>motive</em> — the joiners — is testimony, and
    cannot be proved from a spelling alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    But a deliberate break from a large surname should leave a very particular fingerprint in the
    population a century later: <strong>a tiny name, in the wrong province.</strong> That is precisely what
    is there.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The complication: he may have been signing a z in 1879</h2>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0;max-width:520px">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1879-groom-signature.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Extreme magnification of an 1879 signature surname reading Booij, then an ambiguous letter, then en." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The groom's own signature on the 1879 marriage register, at maximum available
      magnification. The clerk, on the same page, wrote <strong>Booysen</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    He signed what reads as <strong>"Booij_en"</strong> — the older Dutch <strong>ij</strong> digraph, with
    a medial letter whose descending loop is more consistent with <strong>z</strong> than <strong>s</strong>.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    If that reading is right, the z was already his own hand in <strong>1879</strong> — twenty years before
    the war — and the testimony explains a <em>motive</em> for something that had already happened.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">Not established</span> The reading is <strong>not certain</strong>. Cursive Dutch
    <em>s</em> and <em>z</em> are genuinely confusable, and this is a photograph of a duplicate register
    rather than the original. The archive states both readings and prefers neither.
  </p>
  <p>
    Note what it would and would not overturn. It would not touch the <strong>distribution evidence</strong>
    below — the name would still be a tiny, Gauteng-concentrated break from a Cape surname. It would move
    the <em>date</em> of the break back a generation, and make the joiners a remembered reason rather than
    the original one. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The register &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>How rare Booyzen is</h2>
  <p>
    Forebears, accessed 9 September 2026: <strong>646 bearers worldwide</strong>, the
    <strong>507,320th</strong> commonest surname on earth, an incidence of
    <strong>1 in 11,281,031</strong>. Ninety-six per cent are in Africa.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Bearers</th><th>Incidence</th><th>Rank in country</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{zrows.map(([c, b, i, r]) => (
        <tr><td>{c}</td><td class="m">{b}</td><td class="m">{i}</td><td class="m">{r}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap">Note the sixteen in <strong>Australia</strong>. David Defranceski's own family is in
    Brisbane; the Booyzen descent reached it through the Lerena and Defranceski marriages rather than
    under the Booyzen name, so those sixteen are likely to be a separate migration. Unchecked.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>And how common Booysen is</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Bearers</th><th>Incidence</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{srows.map(([c, b, i, r]) => (
        <tr><td>{c}</td><td class="m">{b}</td><td class="m">{i}</td><td class="m">{r}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">
    <strong>53,764 worldwide</strong>, the 10,459th commonest surname on earth, and
    <strong>52,009 of them in South Africa</strong> — one South African in a thousand.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <strong>Booysen is 83 times commoner than Booyzen.</strong><br />
    In South Africa the gap is wider still: <strong>1 in 1,042</strong> against <strong>1 in 87,951</strong>
    — a factor of <strong>84</strong>.
  </blockquote>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The finding: the two names live in different provinces</h2>
  <p>This is the part that is hard to explain any other way.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Province</th><th>Share of <em>Booysen</em></th><th>Share of <em>Booyzen</em></th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Western Cape</strong></td><td class="m">39%</td><td class="m">10%</td>
          <td>the parent name's heartland</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Eastern Cape</strong></td><td class="m">17%</td><td class="m">—</td>
          <td>the old Cape Colony districts</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gauteng</strong></td><td class="m">15%</td><td class="m"><strong>45%</strong></td>
          <td><strong>the Witwatersrand — Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Limpopo</td><td class="m">—</td><td class="m">9%</td><td>—</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><b>Booysen is a Cape surname.</b> Fifty-six per cent of its South African bearers are in the
      Western and Eastern Cape, which is where the family arrived in the seventeenth century and stayed.</p>
    <p><b>Booyzen is a Johannesburg surname.</b> Nearly half of all the Booyzens in the world are in
      <b>Gauteng</b> — a province that did not exist as a settlement until 1886.</p>
    <p>Now set that against the testimony, which was recorded without any reference to statistics:
      a family in the <b>Eastern Cape</b> changes its spelling during the war, is burned out, and
      <b>puts itself on a train to Johannesburg</b>, where the boys go onto the mines.</p>
    <blockquote>
      The surname data was not collected to test that story, and cannot have been shaped by it. It says
      the same thing: <strong>a small group broke off a Cape name and reproduced on the Rand.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> This is corroboration, not proof. Distribution cannot show
      <em>why</em> a spelling changed, and 616 people is a small enough number that founder effects and
      simple clerical drift both remain live explanations. What it does establish is that
      <b>Booyzen behaves like a young name with a single origin</b>, not like a long-standing spelling
      variant scattered through the Cape alongside its parent.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the name means</h2>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A patronymic</h3>
      <p><b>Booysen</b> is Dutch and means <b>"son of Booy"</b>. <em>Booy</em> is itself a short form of
        <b>Boudewijn</b> — the Dutch form of <b>Baldwin</b>, from Germanic <em>bald</em> ("bold") and
        <em>win</em> ("friend"). The English cousin of the name is <b>Boyce</b>; the North German and
        Danish is <b>Boysen</b>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A progenitor</h3>
      <p>The South African family descends from <b>Boij Boijeszoon</b> — modernised to <b>Boy Booysen</b> —
        who entered <b>Dutch East India Company</b> service giving his home as <b>Barlt, in Northern
        Friesland, Schleswig-Holstein</b>, and settled at <b>Stellenbosch and Drakenstein</b> in the
        mid-seventeenth century.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    If that progenitor is right, then this family is <b>German or Frisian on the male line as well</b> —
    and joins <b>Kolbe</b> of Württemberg, <b>Schleher</b>, and the German Lutheran community of Soho on
    the female lines. <b>A family that reads as Afrikaans and English is, underneath, substantially
    German.</b> <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> The link from Gerthardus Lodewikus Booysen to Boy
    Booysen is <b>not demonstrated in this archive</b>. <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>Where the line stops &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The family could not spell it either</h2>
  <p class="dek">These four spellings all appear inside this one family's records, sometimes among
    brothers and sisters.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Spelling</th><th>Where it appears here</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m"><strong>Booysen</strong></td><td>the parent name; used by Gerthardus Lodewikus, both Petrus Jacobuses, and most of the nineteenth-century siblings</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><strong>Booyzen</strong></td><td>Willem Hermanus from the name change onward; the whole line since</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><strong>Booÿsen</strong></td><td><strong>Frederik Fortsenatus Booÿsen</strong>, b. 1899 — with the diaeresis, the older Cape Dutch orthography, in the year the war began</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><strong>Kolbe-Booysen</strong></td><td>a compound recorded for Maria Jacoba Smit, who married David Kolbe Booyzen in 1921</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">Within a single sibling group the tree records <b>Jacobus Nicolaas Booysen</b>
    (b. 1840) and <b>Jacobus Nicolaas Booyzen</b> (b. 1841), both dying on <b>17 November 1882</b>. That is
    almost certainly <b>one man entered twice</b> under two spellings — a small demonstration of how
    easily this name splits. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a></p>
  <p class="cap">Sources: Forebears (Booyzen; Booysen), accessed 9 September 2026; Wikipedia,
    <em>Booysen</em>; the MyHeritage tree <em>Defranceski Family Site (23andMe)</em>. Forebears figures are
    modelled estimates from name-frequency data, not a census, and should be read as orders of magnitude.</p>
</section>
</Base>
